# Choose a Trellis communication surface

Trellis RPCs, operations, events, and feeds use NATS with contract-derived
authorization. This describes Trellis surfaces, not a prohibition on other
application networking such as calling an external HTTP API. Generated SDKs
derive subjects and signed envelopes; callers use the typed methods rather than
constructing raw NATS subjects.

| Surface   | Use it when                                                  | What the caller receives                                 |
| --------- | ------------------------------------------------------------ | -------------------------------------------------------- |
| RPC       | One bounded request needs one answer.                        | A typed response or error.                               |
| Operation | The caller starts work whose progress and completion matter. | An operation reference, progress, and a terminal result. |
| Event     | A service announces a fact to authorized subscribers.        | Typed event deliveries, not a request result.            |
| Feed      | A caller needs a filtered, authorized live view.             | A cancellable stream of typed frames.                    |

## RPCs (request/reply)

An RPC waits for one result within a timeout. For the tutorial's `Orders.Create`
declaration, a generated caller uses `client.ordersCreate({ customerId })` and
the service registers `service.handleOrdersCreate(...)`. Expected failures use
the declared error model and `Result` rather than a fabricated success payload.

A timeout means the caller did not receive a result; it does not prove that the
provider did nothing. Application mutations that must be safe to retry need an
application-owned idempotency policy. Trellis does not choose that policy or
your database schema.

## Operations (async workflows)

Operations are caller-visible work with typed progress and a terminal result.
The caller starts an operation and can use its returned handle to watch or wait
for completion. Durable operation state allows observation to resume after a
disconnect. Operation IDs and references are not authorization grants.

Starting, observing, cancelling, and sending named signals are distinct actions.
Declare the appropriate selections in the participant and obtain authority for
them. See [TypeScript operations](/guides/using-operations-ts) and
[Rust operations](/guides/using-operations-rust) for builders and lifecycle
APIs.

Jobs are different: they are service-private execution, such as retries or
background processing behind an operation. Callers observe the operation, not a
private job queue.

## Events (JetStream pub/sub)

Events announce facts. A publish result does not mean that every subscriber
finished handling the event. Live listeners receive new events; a declared
durable event consumer gives a service a replay/redelivery cursor. Durable
processing can deliver an event more than once, so side effects need to tolerate
duplicates or use an inbox transaction to suppress them.

A participant's `subscribe event` selection authorizes the event surface; it
does not create a durable consumer. Declare the consumer separately in IDL when
you need durable processing. If a SQL mutation and an outgoing event must commit
atomically, enqueue the event with that mutation in a SQL outbox transaction.
Neither the inbox nor the outbox chooses the rest of the application's storage
design.

Events are event-type authorized. A general event subscription does not enforce
per-object rules such as "only updates for devices this user owns". Use a feed
whose provider evaluates those rules when the audience needs caller-specific
access.

## Feeds (authorized live views)

A feed takes typed caller input and streams frames until completion or
cancellation. The provider checks the authenticated caller and application
filter; a feed grant alone does not establish ownership of every requested
object. The generated caller supplies an abort signal, and the provider releases
its upstream resources when that signal is cancelled.

The provider may read application events, a database, or another data source.
The feed is the caller-visible authorization boundary, not durable event replay.
For a current value plus changes with durable revisions, consider
[state](/guides/using-state-ts).

## Cross-API dependencies

The project resolves API dependencies before code generation. A participant
selects the actions it needs from those APIs:

```trellis
participant "acme.orders-caller" app {
  use required orders "acme.orders@v1" {
    call rpc "Orders.Create";
  }
}
```

Use `use optional` when the participant can be authorized without the selected
actions. The referenced API still needs to resolve at compilation; optional
authority is not an optional package download or an availability guarantee.
Regenerate after changing selections and review the resulting authority needs.

Generated methods describe possible calls, not current permission. A caller
still needs accepted authority for an action. Service bootstrap can wait while
required authority or dependency evidence is unresolved; inspect the reported
blocker instead of repeatedly changing application code.

## Surface availability

Authorization and availability are separate. A caller can be authorized while no
provider is connected. The advisory `Trellis.Surface.Status` RPC can distinguish
an unknown API/surface, unauthorized access, and an available or unavailable
provider. Select it through the Trellis Core API and use its generated request
model from [/api](/api). Its response does not grant authority or guarantee that
a subsequent request will succeed.
